# Native task and milestone recording

Use this contract for engineering work with an explicitly selected native capture
state. Record tasks and milestones in the native Session by default. Use the
[legacy progress contract](legacy-progress-recording.md) only when the legacy
Evidence workflow was explicitly selected. Pick one recording path per milestone.

## Verify the binding

For an installation that uses native capture by default, run this from the
current code worktree:

```sh
formsy capture context --repo <absolute-code-worktree>
```

This command resolves the current session from the private installation
configuration and host `CODEX_THREAD_ID` or `CODEX_SESSION_ID`. It can establish
separate local state for an authorized new source. Task creation and upload are
separate operations. Use the current session's identity. If resolution fails,
retain the gap. Use `--config <absolute-path>` only with configuration supplied
by the user or identified by a trusted installation receipt. Upload authorization
must come from the user.

On `status=bound` or `status=awaiting_binding`, retain `state`, `source_session_id`, `session_id`,
`repository`, `repository_identity`, `worktree_id`, and `context_revision`
from the receipt. Use those values in subsequent calls. `awaiting_binding` means
the execution target is not yet selected; Task entry associates it automatically.

For workflow development, enter a new request with
`formsy flow enter PROJECT --feature specs/FEATURE --request-file FILE --worktree TARGET`.
For the same target in another worktree or a retained run, use
`formsy flow select PROJECT --run-id RUN --worktree TARGET`.
PROJECT retains the run's workflow materials; TARGET is the existing Git worktree
used for this operation. Prepare a missing worktree before passing its path.
The entry creates or resumes the original Task and associates TARGET. Read back
the same source and Task before continuing. Do not create a separate Task before
flow entry or add a separate capture bind/task-scope-attach step.
Keep the original Task across A→B→A for the same deliverable; use the lifecycle
contract for a separate deliverable. A worktree switch within the active Task
does not require a new user Activity reference. Complete entry and receipt
verification in a separate tool call before operating on the target. Each tool
call operates on one worktree; combining entry and target operations in one call
leaves that call and its late results attributed to its original scope.

Outside the workflow, run `formsy context` as shown below and follow the task
lifecycle contract. To continue the same Task in another worktree, use
`task-switch --task TASK --repo TARGET`; that entry also handles association.
Read back the successful task selection before implementing, testing, or committing.

If capture lacks configuration, the source conflicts, or the repository differs,
report the safe error code and attribution gap. Preserve the installation scope
and existing history. Verify the actual Session, Task, revision and operation target
provided by the Hook. When no target is recorded, select it from the actual operation;
the Session's startup directory does not establish its execution target.

Use the standalone `formsy` executable, private state directory, and actual
Codex source session ID supplied by the user or trusted project configuration.
Use a capture directory already enabled for this session. Across containers,
reuse the same state and original log mounts. Preserve source identity, cursor,
and logs. Skill installation, login, and AWR handoff data each carry their own
scope. Verify native capture authorization and project binding separately.

Run `formsy context --state <directory> --source-session <actual-source-session-id>`.
Match the source to this session and inspect `active_task_id`, task title,
`context_revision`, and `tasks`. Keep tool output to this safe context. If the
trusted state or executable is unavailable, report the gap and continue the
user's authorized work.

## When to record

- Before starting engineering work, switching to a separate deliverable, or
  returning to paused work, compare the actual target to the active task. Follow
  [task boundaries and lifecycle](task-lifecycle.md) and check the action receipt.
  A TaskSegment tracks the specific deliverable. Reuse it for implementation,
  tests, repairs, and commits within that deliverable.
- Record material decisions as `decision`, executed checks and limitations as
  `verification`, real blockers as `blocked`, and stage handoffs as `handoff`.
- The native log collector already records routine conversation and tool events.

```sh
formsy record --state <directory> --source-session <source-id> \
  --id <milestone-retry-key> --kind verification --text '<actual-checks-and-limitations>'
```

A retry key starts with an ASCII letter or digit and can contain up to 128 bytes
of ASCII letters, digits, and `_.:-`. Reuse the key and content for the same
retry. Use a new key for a correction or a new milestone. The same key with
different content fails. An exact retry preserves the active task. Retain the
receipt and match task identities directly. Before writing a declaration, task
transitions and `record` drain the fully written source log through the source
watermark. Late tool results retain the original call's attribution. A partial
line or read failure prevents the declaration.

The declaration remains `caller_asserted`. Include the executed command,
observed result, and scope. Report unknown source quotations or code versions
as gaps. Keep historical task starts and AWR progress separate from the native
receipt. Summarize the actual target, progress, and gaps to the user.

## Authorized tool bodies

Task transitions, `record`, and `milestone-context` drain the source log.
When the user expressly authorizes capture of tool arguments and return bodies
for this call, verify the code repository scope and add
`--repo <code-repository> --allow-tool-body`. Authorization applies to the
individual call. Server reading controls, Skill installation, and earlier
approvals carry separate scope.

Use metadata mode when this approval is unavailable. Previously omitted
historical bodies stay omitted. Results keep the original call's task
attribution. A host message can leave execution state unknown. Use an actual
exit code or test result as execution evidence.

## Attach exact source references

After recording a milestone, use the newer CLI to look up any exact captured
user or assistant messages it refers to:

```sh
formsy activity-index --state <directory> --source-session <source-id> --after <sequence> --limit 50
```

The default `after` is 0 and the maximum `limit` is 100. Continue while
`next_after` is present. This query returns identity, sequence, source event
position, type, and task identity. It leaves source bodies and capture state
alone. Choose the Activity ID using the actual message's source position or a
verified original Web activity link. Leave the reference missing if the exact
message cannot be established.

```sh
formsy milestone-context --state <directory> --source-session <source-id> \
  --id <reference-retry-key> --milestone <record-receipt-activity-id> \
  --source-activities <exact-message-id[,exact-message-id]> --repo <code-repository>
```

Here `--repo` identifies the code repository used to read origin identity.
Supply up to 32 `source_recorded` user or assistant message references from the
same session. The command drains fully written source events, then rejects
missing references, wrong types, or conflicting retries. A failure leaves the
original declaration intact. Each reference is stored in a separate immutable
MilestoneContext. The current version supports message references and preserves
source text. After migration to state version 3, continue using the newer CLI.

The declaration and referenced source have separate navigation. A reference
records identity. Verify relevance, execution, and task completion independently.
Until the Server ingests the reference, report it as pending synchronization.

## Upload and confirmation

A local receipt proves persistence. After the user expressly authorizes native
session upload and the destination repository has been verified, run:

```sh
formsy sync --state <directory> --repo <dedicated-data-git-directory> --remote <authorized-destination> --allow-upload
```

Sync exports existing activities and transfers data refs. Collect subsequent
messages separately. Use the existing Git credential helper and preserve the
authorized destination and scope.

If private state contains tool observations, synchronization also requires
express authorization to upload tool bodies for this call and the
`--allow-tool-upload` flag. Collection and upload require separate approval.
If upload approval is missing, keep the state local and report the pending
authorization.

Use foreground `watch` when the user expressly requests continuous collection
or upload. Use `--capture-repo <code-repository>` for the code capture scope
and `--repo <dedicated-data-git-directory>` for data transfer. Tool body capture
requires `--allow-tool-body`. Transfer uses `--remote <authorized-destination>
--allow-upload --allow-tool-upload` with the corresponding approvals. These
approvals apply to that foreground run. Installing background work or widening
historical coverage requires a separate explicit request.

Watch detects reference-only changes and keeps references separate from
declarations. After an exit, verify the binding and authorization before
restarting. Retain queue receipts and run a single collector.

`git_uploaded=true` records Git OID confirmation. Verify Server ingestion using
the authorized project's sync result and the original Web task or milestone
view. If the Server receipt is unavailable, report upload as complete and
ingestion as pending. Keep local records on failure and report the failure.
Background upload requires explicit setup. Skill invocation varies by turn.
